= 42
{parent=Section D}
{scope}

= a
{parent=42}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <gyromagnetic ratio> is the signed proportionality coefficient $\boldsymbol\mu=\gamma\mathbf I$ between a <nuclear magnetic moment> and its <spin angular momentum>. With the angular-frequency convention, $\omega_0=|\gamma|B_0$, and $\gamma$ is expressed in rad s$^{-1}$ T$^{-1}$.

= b
{parent=42}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For a positive <gyromagnetic ratio> the <magnetic moment> is parallel to the <spin angular momentum>; for a negative ratio it is antiparallel. The <Hamiltonian> $\hat H_Z=-\gamma B_0\hat I_z$ gives $E_m=-\gamma\hbar B_0m$. Thus **$m=+1/2$ is lower for the proton, whereas $m=-1/2$ is lower for nitrogen-15**. Their <spin> precession senses are opposite for a common positive field convention; measurable <absorption> <frequencies> and energy separations remain positive.

= c
{parent=42}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The energy separation is $\Delta E=\hbar|\gamma|B_0$. At 10 T this gives
$$
\boxed{\Delta E_H=2.816\times10^{-25}\ \mathrm J=1.757\times10^{-6}\ \mathrm{eV}},\qquad
\boxed{\Delta E_N=2.847\times10^{-26}\ \mathrm J=1.777\times10^{-7}\ \mathrm{eV}}.
$$
At 300 K, $k_BT=4.142\times10^{-21}\ \mathrm J=0.02585\ \mathrm{eV}$, so the splitting-to-thermal-energy ratios are $6.80\times10^{-5}$ and $6.87\times10^{-6}$. The two <spin> levels therefore have nearly equal populations. Their equilibrium population <polarizations> are approximately half these ratios, **$3.40\times10^{-5}$ and $3.44\times10^{-6}$**, explaining weak thermal <NMR> sensitivity despite well-defined resonant transition energies.

= d
{parent=42}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

<NMR> probes the chosen <isotope>'s local environments and motion in the bulk without electrical contacts. Line narrowing, relaxation and exchange measurements can expose hopping across a broad range of timescales, separate sites with resolved shifts, and provide microscopic <activation energies>. Pulsed-field-gradient measurements can sometimes measure longer-range displacement directly; local relaxation measurements usually infer it through a model.

Limitations include low sensitivity for dilute or low-abundance nuclei, overlap of inequivalent sites, quadrupolar broadening for <spins> above one-half, and contributions from paramagnetic impurities or other relaxation mechanisms. Heating can change defect populations or phase composition. Most importantly, **local hopping need not produce long-range <ionic conductivity>**: back-and-forth motion, correlated jumps and trapped <ions> can relax <spins> without carrying charge over macroscopic distances. Connecting $T_1$ to a <diffusion coefficient> requires an explicit jump model and comparison with a bulk transport measurement.

= e
{parent=42}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

For an exponentially correlated local field, a single-frequency illustrative relaxation rate is $T_1^{-1}=C\tau_c/(1+\omega_0^2\tau_c^2)$. Its derivative with respect to $\tau_c$ changes sign at $\omega_0\tau_c=1$. Slow motion gives $T_1\propto\tau_c$, and fast motion gives $T_1\propto1/\tau_c$; an <Arrhenius plot> consequently has a **minimum of $T_1$** between opposite-slope limits.

For identical spin-one-half nuclei with homonuclear dipolar relaxation, include both transition <frequencies>:
$$
\frac1{T_1}=C\left[\frac{\tau_c}{1+\omega_0^2\tau_c^2}+\frac{4\tau_c}{1+4\omega_0^2\tau_c^2}\right].
$$
Writing $x=\omega_0\tau_c$, the maximum of the rate satisfies
$$
\frac{1-x^2}{(1+x^2)^2}+4\frac{1-4x^2}{(1+4x^2)^2}=0,
\qquad x=x_*\simeq0.616.
$$
Let $\ell$ be jump length, $\tau_j$ the total waiting time, $\tau_c=c\tau_j$, and $f_D$ the <diffusion jump correlation factor>. In three dimensions $D=f_D\ell^2/(6\tau_j)=f_Dc\ell^2/(6\tau_c)$. Combining this with $D=D_0\exp[-E_D/(k_BT)]$ gives
$$
\boxed{D_0\exp\!\left[-\frac{E_D}{k_BT^*}\right]=\frac{f_Dc\ell^2\omega_0}{6x_*}},\qquad \omega_0=|\gamma|B_0.
$$
The numerical constant is one for the single-frequency illustration and 0.616 for the homonuclear dipolar model. Assumptions are a single activated process, exponential field correlations, a temperature-independent prefactor and coupling amplitude, and an established relation between local correlation and translational motion. Defect creation can enter the measured <activation energy>; it cannot automatically be identified with a bare migration barrier. The fluctuation-spectrum approach originates in the relaxation theory of <Nicolaas Bloembergen>, <Edward Mills Purcell> and <Robert Pound>.<Relaxation Effects in Nuclear Magnetic Resonance Absorption by Nicolaas Bloembergen and others>[Physical Review 73, pp. 679–712: dipolar relaxation and motional correlation]{ref}

= f
{parent=42}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

The fluorine sites form a simple cubic sublattice of spacing $\ell=a/2=3.10\ \mathring{\mathrm A}$. Adopt uncorrelated nearest-neighbour hopping, identify the field-correlation time with the total jump waiting time, and use homonuclear dipolar relaxation: $f_D=c=1$ and $x_*=0.616$. Then
$$
\omega_0=(2.518\times10^8)(0.25)=6.295\times10^7\ \mathrm{s^{-1}},\quad
\tau_c^*=\frac{0.616}{\omega_0}=9.79\times10^{-9}\ \mathrm s,
$$
$$
\boxed{D(T^*)=\frac{(3.10\times10^{-10})^2}{6\tau_c^*}\simeq1.64\times10^{-12}\ \mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}}},\qquad
\boxed{D_0=D(T^*)\exp\!\left[\frac{1.52}{(8.61733\times10^{-5})(926)}\right]\simeq3.1\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}}}.
$$
If the simplified single-frequency condition $\omega_0\tau_c=1$ is used instead, the corresponding estimates are $1.01\times10^{-12}\ \mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}}$ and $1.9\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{m^2\,s^{-1}}$. These are explicit model-dependent estimates, not a uniquely measured tracer diffusivity: a different correlation-time-to-jump-time conversion or defect correlation factor multiplies them by $f_Dc$. The <lattice constant> supplies a jump distance, not proof that all possible neighbouring sites are vacant.

= 43
{parent=Section D}
{scope}

= a
{parent=43}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The conventional lattice-defect description is **vacancy-mediated <diffusion>**: an <ion> moves into a neighbouring vacancy and leaves a vacancy behind. <sodium chloride> is another familiar example. In a real alpha-alumina specimen the dominant species and transport path depend on <oxygen> chemical potential, dopants and grain boundaries, so this label should not be read as a universal microscopic assignment for every aluminium and <oxygen> <diffusion> experiment. A vacancy mechanism is different from an interstitial <ion> moving between otherwise empty interstitial sites.

= b
{parent=43}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Here $\mathbf j$ is electric <current density>, $n$ the <number density> of independently mobile <ions>, $Q$ their charge, $T$ absolute temperature and $k_B$ the <Boltzmann constant>. The sum runs over all nearest-neighbour destinations $j$ of site $i$, and $\mathbf r_{ij}$ is the displacement from $i$ to $j$. The applied field is $\boldsymbol{\mathcal E}$, $\omega_0$ is an attempt <frequency>, and $\Delta E_{ij}$ is the zero-field <reaction energy barrier> for the indicated jump. With the normalization of the supplied equation,
$$
\boxed{f(\omega_0,\Delta E_{ij},T)=\omega_0\exp[-\Delta E_{ij}/(k_BT)]}.
$$
It has units s$^{-1}$; it is not an equilibrium occupation <probability>. The current formula is the linear-response result of a small field-induced asymmetry in forward and reverse rates, requiring $|Q\mathbf r_{ij}\cdot\boldsymbol{\mathcal E}|\ll k_BT$. Use its explicit factor $1/2$ consistently rather than absorbing it into a redefined jump rate.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Expanding the <scalar product> gives $j_\alpha=[nQ^2/(2k_BT)]\sum_j f\,r_{ij,\alpha}\sum_\beta r_{ij,\beta}\mathcal E_\beta$. Comparing coefficients of the independently variable field components yields
$$
\boxed{\sigma_{\alpha\beta}=\frac{nQ^2}{2k_BT}\sum_{j=nn(i)}f(\omega_0,\Delta E_{ij},T)\,r_{ij,\alpha}r_{ij,\beta}}.
$$
The <tensor> is symmetric and <positive semidefinite> because its contraction with any vector twice is a nonnegative sum of squares.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= 1
{parent=iii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

Set $F=\omega_0e^{-\Delta E/(k_BT)}$ and $K=nQ^2F/(2k_BT)$. The six simple cubic neighbour vectors are $\pm a\hat x$, $\pm a\hat y$, $\pm a\hat z$. Each diagonal direction receives $2a^2$, and every off-diagonal product vanishes. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\boldsymbol\sigma_{\rm sc}=2Ka^2 I=\frac{nQ^2Fa^2}{k_BT}I}.
$$
The result uses $a$ as the nearest-neighbour distance specified here, not automatically as the conventional-cell edge for the other lattices.

= 2
{parent=iii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

The eight body-centred cubic neighbour vectors are $(a/\sqrt3)(\pm1,\pm1,\pm1)$ with independent signs. Their diagonal sums are $8a^2/3$ and the sign-paired off-diagonal sums cancel. Hence
$$
\boxed{\boldsymbol\sigma_{\rm bcc}=\frac83Ka^2 I=\frac43\frac{nQ^2Fa^2}{k_BT}I}.
$$
The conventional cubic cell edge is $2a/\sqrt3$ in this comparison; using that edge as the hopping distance would spoil the stipulated equal-distance comparison.

= 3
{parent=iii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

The twelve face-centred cubic neighbours are $(a/\sqrt2)(\pm1,\pm1,0)$ and permutations of the zero coordinate. Eight vectors have a nonzero given component, each contributing $a^2/2$, while off-diagonal terms cancel between signs. Thus
$$
\boxed{\boldsymbol\sigma_{\rm fcc}=4Ka^2 I=2\frac{nQ^2Fa^2}{k_BT}I}.
$$
For a cubic neighbour shell this can also be checked from $\sum_jr_{j,\alpha}r_{j,\beta}=(za^2/3)\delta_{\alpha\beta}$ with <coordination number> $z=12$.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

**Yes, for all three ideal lattices under the stated assumptions.** Cubic symmetry and equal rates make $\boldsymbol\sigma=\sigma I$ with $\sigma>0$, so $\mathbf j=\sigma\boldsymbol{\mathcal E}$. Positive <ions> drift along the field. The current coefficient contains $Q^2$, so negative charge carriers would also produce a conventional current along the field, although their particle drift would reverse. Anisotropic barriers or a field strong enough to invalidate <linear response> would require a new calculation.

= v
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

At fixed <number density>, attempt <frequency>, nearest-neighbour distance and barrier, the conductivities have ratio **$\sigma_{\rm sc}:\sigma_{\rm bcc}:\sigma_{\rm fcc}=1:4/3:2$**. Thus the face-centred cubic array is largest. Numerically,
$$
F=10^{13}\exp\!\left[-\frac{0.35}{(8.61733\times10^{-5})(300)}\right]\simeq1.32\times10^7\ \mathrm{s^{-1}},
$$
$$
\boxed{\sigma_{\rm fcc}=\frac{2nQ^2Fa^2}{k_BT}\simeq1.47\times10^{-7}\ \mathrm{S\,m^{-1}}}.
$$
The small value is consistent with the low supplied carrier <number density> and strongly activated hopping. This comparison holds $n$ fixed rather than comparing fully occupied <crystal structures>, whose site densities at the same neighbour distance differ.

= Section E
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}

= 44
{parent=Section E}
{scope}

= a
{parent=44}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

**<zinc>(II) should bind more strongly to 1.** The <macrocycle> presents six <amine> donors that can chelate the dication in a roughly octahedral environment. Multiple donor contacts, the <chelate effect> and preorganization favour this binding. <lithium> is small and strongly prefers an appropriately sized oxygen-rich pocket; it gains less from this large nitrogen-donor cavity. Charge density alone is insufficient to rank the complexes: donor type, geometry, solvation and the strain of wrapping the host around each <ion> also matter.

= b
{parent=44}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The predominant self-sorted distribution is **$[\mathrm{Zn}(1)]^{2+}$, $[\mathrm{Li}(2)]^+$ and free 3**, so the reporter remains mainly in its **554 nm** state. <zinc> benefits from the six <amine> donors of 1 without forcing 2 into its strained zinc-binding conformation. <lithium> occupies 2 because its <lithium> affinity exceeds that of 3. Each metal is consumed in the preferred complex, leaving the reporter unoccupied.

This is a dominant-species prediction. A tenfold affinity difference alone would not guarantee quantitative <lithium> sorting if the absolute affinities were weak or if free metal and all competing complexes had appreciable populations. Concentration and <equilibrium constants> are not supplied, so an exact species fraction is not justified.

= c
{parent=44}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

<protonation> of the basic <amine> host weakens its <zinc> binding. <zinc> transfers to 2, displacing <lithium>, which can then bind the crown-like site of 3. The predicted switched distribution is **protonated 1, $[\mathrm{Zn}(2)]^{2+}$ and $[\mathrm{Li}(3)]^+$**, giving **512 nm emission**. The strain penalty for <zinc> binding 2 is accepted when the better competing host is protonated.

Without <fluorescence>, compare $^1$H <NMR> spectra of the mixture with independently prepared free and metal-bound hosts, looking for changes near the <amine>, pyridyl and crown-ether donors. $^7$Li <NMR> can follow <lithium> environments; chemical-shift changes and exchange-dependent line shapes should be interpreted using titrations rather than treating one peak as definitive proof of one complex. <zinc>(II) is diamagnetic, so ordinary <NMR> observation is possible. UV–visible <absorption> can independently track changes in 2 and 3. Acid-only controls for each component test whether direct <protonation> shifts the reporter independently of <lithium>; metal-only and acid titrations test the proposed redistribution. The stipulated one-equivalent response is an idealized acid–base sorting cycle, not a pH measurement in aqueous buffer.

= d
{parent=44}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

<DBU> removes the proton from 1, restoring its zinc-binding ability. <zinc> returns from 2 to 1; the regenerated free 2 captures <lithium> from 3, restoring the preferred initial sorting. Thus **the reporter returns predominantly to 554 nm**, while <DBU> becomes its <conjugate acid> and trifluoroacetate remains as counterion. This assumes the added base is strong enough under the solvent conditions and does not itself compete effectively for either metal. Reversing a <fluorescence> shift without checking speciation would not establish all these transfers on its own.

= e
{parent=44}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The acid initially protonates 1 and switches the system into the lithium-bound reporter state, so **512 nm intensity rises rapidly**. The resulting carboxylate then decarboxylates irreversibly. Its <carbanion> removes the proton from 1, giving neutral waste 5 and restoring the original <zinc>/<lithium> distribution. Thus the 512 nm signal is a **transient pulse followed by recovery to its initial low baseline**; no repeated oscillations follow from one fuel addition.

For first-order <decarboxylation> the surviving fuel-derived carboxylate fraction is $e^{-kt}$ with $k=\ln2/(75\ \mathrm{min})$. If proton exchange and metal redistribution are fast, background is subtracted, and the population of the switched state is proportional to remaining protonated 1, the illustrative recovery is
$$
\boxed{I_{512}(t)=I_{\rm low}+(I_{\rm high}-I_{\rm low})2^{-t/(75\,\mathrm{min})}},\qquad t>0.
$$
Half the initial excess signal remains after 75 min and one quarter after 150 min in that limiting model. Actual binding equilibria can make the intensity nonlinear in remaining fuel; the supplied <half-life> fixes the fuel decay, not an exact reporter-intensity law. Monitor $^1$H <NMR> fuel consumption and waste formation alongside the optical trace to distinguish dissipation-driven recovery from <photobleaching> or simple dilution.

\Image[paper-3-switching.png]
{title=Original limiting-model pulse and recovery after one acid-fuel addition}

= 45
{parent=Section E}
{scope}

= a
{parent=45}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Each primary <amine> arm of 2 condenses with one <aldehyde> 1, eliminating three waters overall and forming a tripodal tris(pyridylimine) ligand $L$. The three <imine> <nitrogens> and the three adjacent pyridine <nitrogens> chelate one Fe(II), giving **$[\mathrm{Fe}L]^{2+}$**, with three outward-facing terminal pyridines still free. The central <tertiary amine> is the tethering apex, not a seventh donor in the six-coordinate model.

The Fe coordination is approximately octahedral and is propeller-chiral: three bidentate chelates can have **$\Delta$ or $\Lambda$ configuration**. Achiral starting components and conditions give a racemic population unless a separate resolving influence is introduced. <imine> condensation and metal coordination cooperate in <subcomponent self-assembly>; the metal template selects the convergent ligand arrangement.

= b
{parent=45}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Each Pd(dppp) unit retains its chelating phosphine and offers two adjacent pyridyl binding sites at an approximately right angle. Three such units bridge pairs of <metalloligands>, giving **$[\mathrm{Fe}_2\mathrm{Pd}_3L_2(\mathrm{dppp})_3]^{10+}$**, a **trigonal bipyramid** with two Fe vertices and three equatorial Pd vertices. All six outward pyridines are used: $2\times3=3\times2$. The Pd/Fe ratio is $3/2$, matching the reagent dose, and the cation charge is $2(2)+3(2)=10$ because the organic ligands are neutral.

The two donor sites left by dppp are cis, so each equatorial Pd joins one arm from each Fe cap. A mere mass-spectral maximum charge is not an independent structure determination, but its consistency with donor counting and binding geometry strongly selects this model.

\Image[paper-3-cages.png]
{title=Original connectivity models for the iron–palladium bipyramid and cube}

= c
{parent=45}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

**An individual bipyramid is <chiral>.** Matching helicities at the two <iron> centres give the homochiral $\Delta\Delta$ and $\Lambda\Lambda$ <enantiomers>, with the three spanning connections winding in opposite senses in the two structures. The idealized molecular framework has a threefold axis but no mirror plane, inversion centre or improper rotation that converts it into itself. Achiral synthesis can give a <racemic mixture> of these <chiral> cages; racemic bulk composition does not make each cage achiral. The corresponding experimentally characterized methyl-substituted Fe–Pd bipyramid contains both homochiral <enantiomers> in its crystal.<Dynamic Complex-to-Complex Transformations of Heterobimetallic Systems Influence the Cage Structure or Spin State of Iron(II) Ions by Matthias Hardy and others>[Figure 1 and discussion of the bipyramidal crystal structure]{ref}

= d
{parent=45}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Without a blocking diphosphine, square-planar Pd(II) can bind four terminal pyridines. If there are $N$ Fe units and $M$ Pd centres, saturation requires $3N=4M$. The given ratio $M/N=0.75$ and charge $2(N+M)=28$ yield **$N=8$, $M=6$**. Thus **$[\mathrm{Fe}_8\mathrm{Pd}_6L_8]^{28+}$** has Fe at the eight cube corners and Pd at the six face centres. Each corner Fe connects through its three arms to the three neighbouring face-centre Pd atoms; each Pd accepts four pyridines around its square face.

There are 24 Fe-unit-to-Pd connections, checked independently as $8\times3=6\times4$. It is useful to distinguish the cube of eight Fe nodes from its dual octahedron of six Pd nodes: both describe the same assembly, with different node sets.

= e
{parent=45}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

**The idealized cage is <chiral>, with <point group> $O$**, rather than $O_h$. The eight <iron> tris-chelate units have the same helicity within one cage; the two <enantiomers> are all-$\Delta$ and all-$\Lambda$. The fourfold axes pass through opposite Pd face centres, and the threefold axes pass through opposite Fe corners. There are also proper twofold rotations through opposite cube-edge midpoints. These proper rotations preserve the local handedness. Mirror planes, inversion and improper rotations would reverse each Fe propeller and are absent from an individual homochiral cage. The proper cubic group has 24 operations: $E$, $8C_3$, $6C_4$, $3C_2$ along the face axes and $6C_2$ along edge axes. Real conformations and crystal packing can lower this idealized molecular symmetry.

= f
{parent=45}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

The six diphosphines bind the six Pd centres, occupying two adjacent sites on each and displacing two pyridines per Pd. Reassembly therefore favours the two-connected Pd nodes of the bipyramid instead of the cube's four-connected nodes. The balanced transformation is
$$
\boxed{[\mathrm{Fe}_8\mathrm{Pd}_6L_8]^{28+}+6\,\mathrm{dppp}\longrightarrow2[\mathrm{Fe}_2\mathrm{Pd}_3L_2(\mathrm{dppp})_3]^{10+}+4[\mathrm{Fe}L]^{2+}}.
$$
Thus **two cages of type 4 and four free <metalloligands> of type 3** form per cage 5. <iron>, <palladium>, ligand and charge balances all close. Strong phosphine <chelation> drives the donor replacement, while the release of additional discrete species can favour entropy. The analogous conversion is observed experimentally in the cited heterobimetallic system.<Dynamic Complex-to-Complex Transformations of Heterobimetallic Systems Influence the Cage Structure or Spin State of Iron(II) Ions by Matthias Hardy and others>[Scheme 3 and discussion of six-equivalent dppp addition]{ref}

= Section F
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}

= 46
{parent=Section F}
{scope}

= a
{parent=46}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Take incidence from the left and set the reactant and product potentials to zero. The asymptotic scattering boundary conditions are $\psi=Ae^{ikx}+Be^{-ikx}$ for $x<0$ and $\psi=Fe^{ikx}$ for $x>a$, with $k=\sqrt{2mE}/\hbar$. There is **no incoming wave from the product side**. At each finite potential step, $\psi$ and $d\psi/dx$ are continuous for the same mass on both sides.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <probability current> is
$$
j=\frac{\hbar}{2mi}\left(\psi^*\psi'-\psi\psi^{*\prime}\right)=\frac\hbar m\operatorname{Im}(\psi^*\psi').
$$
For the incident and transmitted travelling waves, $j_{\rm in}=\hbar k|A|^2/m$ and $j_{\rm tr}=\hbar k|F|^2/m$. Thus **$P(E)=|F/A|^2$** here. If the asymptotic wave numbers differed, the ratio of outgoing to incoming velocities would also be required. Current conservation gives $P+|B/A|^2=1$ for a real nonabsorbing barrier.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Write $\psi=Ce^{i\alpha x}+De^{-i\alpha x}$ inside the barrier. Matching the <wavefunction> and derivative gives the four equations
$$
\boxed{A+B=C+D,\qquad k(A-B)=\alpha(C-D),}
$$
$$
\boxed{Ce^{i\alpha a}+De^{-i\alpha a}=Fe^{ika},\qquad \alpha(Ce^{i\alpha a}-De^{-i\alpha a})=kFe^{ika}.}
$$
For sub-barrier energy the same equations hold by analytic continuation to imaginary $\alpha$. No coefficient solution is needed for these matching conditions.

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= A
{parent=iv}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=A}

For $0<E<V_0$, put $\alpha=i\kappa$ with $\kappa=\sqrt{2m(V_0-E)}/\hbar$. Since $\sin(i\kappa a)=i\sinh(\kappa a)$, both signs in the quotient change and
$$
\boxed{P(E)=\left[1+\frac{V_0^2\sinh^2(\kappa a)}{4E(V_0-E)}\right]^{-1}}.
$$
It is finite and positive for nonzero incident energy and finite width: this is <quantum tunnelling>. In a thick barrier $P\simeq[16E(V_0-E)/V_0^2]e^{-2\kappa a}$. Inside, the scattering wave is a combination $Ce^{-\kappa x}+De^{\kappa x}$, not an isolated real decaying exponential; their complex coefficients allow a nonzero conserved current.

\Image[paper-3-barrier.png]
{title=Original exact matched scattering wavefunctions below and well above a square barrier}

= B
{parent=iv}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=B}

Let $\kappa_0=\sqrt{2mV_0}/\hbar$. As $E\to0^+$, $\kappa\to\kappa_0$ while the large denominator term scales as $1/E$. Hence
$$
\boxed{P(E)\sim\frac{4E}{V_0\sinh^2(\kappa_0a)}\longrightarrow0}.
$$
The limit is linear in incident energy for fixed positive height and nonzero width. A below-barrier wave has nonzero penetration at finite energy, but that does not imply a finite zero-energy transmitted flux.

= C
{parent=iv}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=C}

For $E\gg V_0$, expand the reciprocal to obtain
$$
\boxed{P(E)=1-\frac{V_0^2}{4E^2}\sin^2(\alpha a)+O[(V_0/E)^3]}.
$$
Retain the exact $\alpha$ in the bounded oscillatory factor unless a further phase expansion is controlled. Reflection is small, with an oscillatory interference correction of order $(V_0/E)^2$. The <wavefunction> is nearly a travelling wave throughout; its wave number inside is slightly smaller than outside, so its <wavelength> is slightly larger there. The reflected wave adds weak standing-wave modulation on the incident side.

= D
{parent=iv}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=D}

For fixed $V_0$ and $a$, $0\leq\sin^2(\alpha a)\leq1$ while the multiplier of this bounded factor tends to zero as $E^{-2}$. Consequently **$\boxed{\lim_{E\to\infty}P(E)=1}$**, even though the sine factor itself does not have a limit. This is the high-energy classical transmission limit.

= v
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

The classical <transmission probability> is **$P_{\rm cl}(E)=\Theta(E-V_0)$**; the convention exactly at the threshold does not affect a thermal integral. Below the barrier it is zero, whereas the quantum result permits tunnelling. Above the barrier it is one, whereas quantum reflection is generally nonzero except at transmission resonances. Both descriptions tend to unit transmission at arbitrarily high energy, and both have zero transmitted <probability> in the zero-energy limit for this positive barrier.

= vi
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=vi}

With $\beta=1/(k_BT)$ and a consistently normalized reactant <partition function> $Q_r$, the one-dimensional channel formula is
$$
k(T)Q_r=\frac1{2\pi\hbar}\int_0^\infty e^{-\beta E}P(E)\,dE.
$$
Classically the integral starts at $V_0$, giving
$$
\boxed{k_{\rm cl}(T)=\frac{e^{-\beta V_0}}{2\pi\hbar\beta Q_r}}.
$$
An infinite free reactant half-line has no finite canonical population, so define a reactant normalization length $L$, for which $Q_r=L\sqrt{m/(2\pi\beta\hbar^2)}$. Then **$k_{\rm cl}=L^{-1}\sqrt{k_BT/(2\pi m)}\,e^{-V_0/(k_BT)}$**. Equivalently $kL$ is a one-dimensional thermal flux per unit reactant density, with dimensions of velocity. A <rate constant> without either this normalization or a specified reactant well is not dimensionally complete.

= b
{parent=46}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Choose a <reaction coordinate> $s(q)$ that is negative in the reactant region and positive in the product region; $s=0$ defines a <dividing surface>. With unnormalized canonical phase-space averaging, define
$$
C_{fs}(t)=\frac1{(2\pi\hbar)^f}\int dq\,dp\,e^{-\beta H(q,p)}\,\delta[s(q)]\dot s(q,p)\Theta[s(q_t)].
$$
The signed initial flux $\delta(s)\dot s$ counts both directions, while the later side function tests product commitment. The rate is **$k=C_{fs}(t_{\rm pl})/Q_r$** on a plateau after microscopic recrossings have died away but before reactant depletion and global equilibration become important. The $t\to0^+$ limit instead gives the transition-state-theory positive flux and can overcount trajectories that later return. A suitable <dividing surface> changes that early flux but not a correctly evaluated committed plateau rate. This correlation formulation provides the dynamical correction to a transition-state estimate.<Statistical mechanics of isomerization dynamics in liquids and the transition state approximation by David Chandler>[Journal of Chemical Physics 68, pp. 2959–2970: rate correlation functions and recrossing]{ref}

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= I
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=I}

Take $s=x-x^\ddagger$ with $0<x^\ddagger<a$. A trajectory crossing to the right at the barrier top goes downhill on exit and cannot return in this model. Its initial Boltzmann weight contains $e^{-\beta V_0}$; negative-velocity trajectories are on the reactant side at the commitment time. Therefore
$$
C_{fs}^{\rm top}=\frac{e^{-\beta V_0}}{2\pi\hbar}\int_0^\infty\frac p m e^{-\beta p^2/(2m)}dp
=\boxed{\frac{e^{-\beta V_0}}{2\pi\hbar\beta}}.
$$
Dividing by the same $Q_r$ gives the result in the energy-integral calculation. No width appears in the classical rate because every classically allowed trajectory passes through the flat barrier.

= II
{parent=ii}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=II}

Put the <dividing surface> at $x^\ddagger<0$. The immediate positive flux includes all positive momenta and gives $C_{fs}(0^+)=1/(2\pi\hbar\beta)$, an overestimate. Trajectories with $p^2/(2m)<V_0$ hit the barrier, return across this surface, and cease contributing to the later product-side indicator. After their recrossing transient, only $p>\sqrt{2mV_0}$ survives:
$$
C_{fs}^{\rm plateau}=\frac1{2\pi\hbar}\int_{\sqrt{2mV_0}}^\infty\frac p m e^{-\beta p^2/(2m)}dp
=\boxed{\frac{e^{-\beta V_0}}{2\pi\hbar\beta}}.
$$
The exact rate is again unchanged; the transmission correction to the reactant-side transition-state estimate is $e^{-\beta V_0}$. The return time depends on surface placement and velocity, so the plateau means a timescale-separated limiting flux, not the claim that every trajectory has one identical finite recrossing time.

= 47
{parent=Section F}
{scope}

= a
{parent=47}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $z_{ij}$ be the input to output weight $w_j$ for datum $i$, including $z_{i0}=1$ for the bias. With identity output activation, $h_i=a_i=\sum_jw_jz_{ij}$. The <chain rule> gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{\partial E_i}{\partial w_j}=(h_i-y_i)z_{ij}}.
$$
The $z_{ij}$ are held fixed when differentiating an output-layer weight; they may be hidden-layer activations rather than raw descriptors. Hidden-weight derivatives require <backpropagation> through the preceding layers.

= b
{parent=47}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The printed PDF has a **plus sign before the second logarithm**. Differentiating that expression literally, with $h_i=\sigma(a_i)$ and $\partial h_i/\partial w_j=h_i(1-h_i)z_{ij}$, gives
$$
\frac{\partial E_i}{\partial h_i}=-\frac{y_i}{h_i}-\frac{1-y_i}{1-h_i},\qquad
\boxed{\frac{\partial E_i}{\partial w_j}=-[y_i(1-h_i)+(1-y_i)h_i]z_{ij}}.
$$
For example, at $y_i=0$ it is $-h_i z_{ij}$, so minimizing this printed objective pushes the predicted <probability> of class one upwards rather than downwards; the objective is unbounded below as $h_i\to1$. It is therefore not the usual <binary cross-entropy>.

If the second sign was intended to be negative, the standard negative log-likelihood is $-y_i\log h_i-(1-y_i)\log(1-h_i)$ and the derivative simplifies instead to **$(h_i-y_i)z_{ij}$**. Stating both cases preserves the actual question's algebra while identifying the apparent sign error. The logarithms here are natural; another base multiplies the <gradients> by its constant conversion factor.

= c
{parent=47}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For $K$ mutually exclusive classes, the output layer should provide $K$ nonnegative <probabilities> summing to one, with labels aligned consistently to output coordinates. A softmax activation on <logits> $a_k$ provides
$$
\boxed{p_k=\frac{e^{a_k}}{\sum_{\ell=1}^K e^{a_\ell}}},\qquad \sum_kp_k=1.
$$
Subtracting $\max_\ell a_\ell$ from all <logits> before exponentiation preserves these <probabilities> and prevents numerical overflow. Categorical <cross-entropy> $-\sum_ky_k\log p_k$ is appropriate for one-hot or probabilistic class targets. Independent sigmoid outputs fit a different setting in which multiple labels can be present simultaneously and their <probabilities> need not sum to one.

= d
{parent=47}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Compose <affine maps> with nonlinear hidden <activation functions>, for example $h(x)=W_2\operatorname{ReLU}(W_1x+b_1)+b_2$. Several hidden units can respond to different linear combinations of input features; later units combine their responses to represent curved or piecewise-linear decision boundaries and interactions. A product interaction can be understood from $xy=[(x+y)^2-(x-y)^2]/4$: units approximating nonlinear functions of $x+y$ and $x-y$ can represent effects that no single additive linear fit captures.

**Nonlinearity in hidden layers is essential:** with only identity activations, repeated <affine maps> collapse to one <affine map> regardless of depth. Width and depth determine available representations; <regularization>, validation and suitable optimization are needed to learn useful rather than overfitted interactions. A nonlinear output activation alone applied to one linear score does not give an unrestricted model of <feature interactions>.

= e
{parent=47}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

A <multilayer perceptron> usually connects every input unit of a layer to every output unit. For $n$ inputs and $m$ outputs its dense affine layer has **$nm+m$ parameters**, including biases. A convolutional layer instead applies small local filters with <weight sharing> across positions. A two-dimensional filter of size $k_h\times k_w$, with $C_{\rm in}$ input channels and $C_{\rm out}$ output channels, has **$k_hk_wC_{\rm in}C_{\rm out}+C_{\rm out}$ parameters**. The count does not increase with spatial height and width, although the activation storage and arithmetic do.

Local <receptive fields> and repeated layers capture increasingly extended patterns. <translation equivariance> follows from shared filters under compatible boundary conventions; pooling or global aggregation can help build invariance but a convolution alone is not generally translation-invariant. Flattening a large convolutional output into a dense head can reintroduce many parameters. For an unordered descriptor vector, a convolutional locality assumption must be justified rather than imposed merely because convolutional layers have fewer weights.

= Section G
{parent=Paper 3}
{scope}

= 48
{parent=Section G}
{scope}

= a
{parent=48}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The free allylic <alcohol> directs epoxidation of the adjacent <alkene> rather than the remote double bond. Applying the D-(−)-tartrate mnemonic gives **the <epoxide> <oxygen> on the back face in the source orientation**, with both new C–O bonds dashed if the original six-membered ring is kept in the plane. The CH$_2$OH substituent at the lower <epoxide> <carbon> consequently projects towards the front in an explicit tetrahedral product drawing. The remote double bond is retained. The opposite tartrate would reverse both newly created <epoxide> <stereocentres>; it would not alter the location of the epoxidized <alkene>.

= b
{parent=48}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Number the epoxide-bearing ring <carbon> carrying CH$_2$OH as C1, then proceed through the other <epoxide> <carbon> C2 around the ring to C6. Heating with water opens the <epoxide> at the less substituted C2 by backside attack: the incoming <oxygen> becomes C2–OH on the front face, while the original <epoxide> <oxygen> remains as C1–OH on the back face. Proton transfers complete formation of the triol. Thus the two original <epoxide> <oxygens>' roles are distinct: one is retained from B and the new hydroxyl <oxygen> comes from water.

Iodine next activates the remaining C4=C5 bond as an iodonium <ion>. The pendant <primary alcohol> <oxygen> attacks intramolecularly at C5 to make the five-membered cyclic <ether> with path **O–CH$_2$–C1–C6–C5**, opening the iodonium anti and leaving iodine at C4 on the opposite face. Bicarbonate removes the oxonium proton and neutralizes acid; it is not the source of the <ether> <oxygen>. The folded tether selects the face of closure compatible with the bridged structure, accounting for the <ether>/iodine <stereochemistry>.

The remaining two hydroxyl groups react with acid-activated 2,2-dimethoxypropane. Successive <oxygen> attack, methanol departure and intramolecular closure form the **isopropylidene <ketal>** of C, protecting that diol without changing its <carbon> configurations. The terminal primary hydroxyl has already become the cyclic <ether> and is not one of the two acetonide <oxygens>. This atom tracking distinguishes the <epoxide> <hydrolysis>, <iodoetherification> and protection stages.

= c
{parent=48}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Silver(I) promotes iodide departure, with precipitation of AgI. The <oxygen> of <dimethyl sulfoxide> substitutes at the iodine-bearing <carbon> to form an alkoxydimethylsulfonium intermediate. Triethylamine removes the <hydrogen> on that <carbon>; collapse forms C=O and breaks O–S, releasing dimethyl sulfide. The result is **<ketone> D by a <Kornblum oxidation>**. In arrow terms, the C–H <electrons> form the <carbonyl> <pi bond> as the O–S bond breaks. The <carbon> bearing iodine becomes the <carbonyl> <carbon>; the existing bridged <ether> and acetonide are spectators. The iodine centre loses its tetrahedral <stereochemistry> because it becomes trigonal planar.

= d
{parent=48}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

A suitable sequence is **selective alpha-bromination, then <enol> O-methylation**. Use one equivalent of Br$_2$ under controlled acidic <enolization> conditions to introduce bromine at the enolizable alpha <carbon> that becomes the brominated vinyl <carbon>. The other alpha site is a constrained bridgehead and cannot form the required ordinary <enol> double bond. Then O-alkylate the <ketone> with <trimethyloxonium tetrafluoroborate> and remove the brominated alpha proton with a nonnucleophilic base such as triethylamine. Collapse of the O-methylated <carbonyl> oxonium gives **the alpha-bromo methyl <enol ether> E**.

The methyl group belongs on <oxygen>, not on the alpha <carbon>. Simply writing methyl iodide with an unspecified <enolate> would not guarantee this outcome because competitive C-alkylation is possible. Use conditions designed for <carbonyl> O-alkylation followed by <deprotonation>, and limit bromine to avoid overbromination or damage to the <protecting group>.

= e
{parent=48}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

This is an **acid-catalysed <alpha-ketol rearrangement>**. Label the original <ketone> <carbon> $C_A$ and the adjacent <tertiary alcohol> <carbon> $C_B$. Protonate the <oxygen> on $C_A$ to activate that <carbonyl>. The appropriately aligned cage C–C <sigma bond> attached to $C_B$ migrates onto $C_A$, while the hydroxyl <oxygen> at $C_B$ supplies its lone pair to form the new $C_B=O$ bond. At the same time the old $C_A=O$ <pi bond> moves onto <oxygen>, which becomes the new $C_A$–OH. <deprotonation> regenerates the acid.

The migration reroutes the bridge rather than removing water: **the original <alcohol> <carbon> becomes the <ketone>, and the original <ketone> <carbon> becomes the <tertiary alcohol>**. A productive conformation aligns the migrating sigma orbital with the accepting <carbonyl> pi-star orbital; the <oxygen> lone pair simultaneously overlaps the developing <p orbital> at the donor <carbon>. The cage's geometry controls which adjacent <sigma bond> has the necessary alignment and which less strained skeletal isomer results. A freely rotating isolated <carbocation> or a simple proton shift cannot account for the changed cage connectivity.

\Image[paper-3-ketol.png]
{title=Original atom-tracked alpha-ketol migration and orbital-alignment model}

= f
{parent=48}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

Manganese(III) removes one <electron> from the <enol>/<enolate> of H, and loss of its hydroxyl proton gives a **carbon-centred <radical> at the tether-bearing alpha <carbon> of the resulting 1,3-dicarbonyl system**. The enolic OH becomes the second <carbonyl> <oxygen>. This <radical> attacks the terminal CH$_2$ of the tethered isopropenyl group, forming a new C–C bond and a six-membered ring. The residual <radical> is on the substituted <carbon> bearing methyl, which is more stable than the primary <radical> from the alternative regiochemical closure.

Dioxygen traps that <radical> as a peroxyl intermediate. <reduction>/proton transfer and O–O cleavage in the ensuing oxygenation/workup sequence give the **new <tertiary alcohol> on the methyl-bearing <carbon>**. The drawn net transformation specifies that oxygenated product, not a persistent <hydroperoxide>; individual <electron>/proton transfers require the reaction medium and workup and cannot be fixed uniquely from the abbreviated reagent line alone. The rigid cage and folded tether select the closure face and therefore the relative configuration of the new ring junction.

Finally the new hydroxyl <oxygen> attacks silicon of <TBSCl>, with imidazole assisting <deprotonation> and chloride departure, giving the **tertiary <silyl ether> I**. The hindered pre-existing hydroxyls remain as shown. Track the two key atom changes: the terminal <alkene> <carbon> becomes a methylene in the new ring, and the former substituted <alkene> <carbon> acquires <oxygen> and retains its methyl group. Calling the first step a two-electron aldol reaction would miss both the single-electron oxidant and the role of dioxygen.

= 49
{parent=Section G}
{scope}

= a
{parent=49}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A route from the supplied cyclohexenone starts with **conjugate methyl addition**. Form an <organocuprate> from methylmagnesium bromide and Cu(I), or use catalytic Cu(I) with controlled methylmagnesium addition, to obtain 3-methylcyclohexanone after <protonation>. <copper> directs 1,4 rather than direct 1,2 addition, so the <ketone> remains intact.

Generate its less hindered <kinetic enolate> at the alpha <carbon> **opposite the methyl-bearing side**, using LDA at low temperature, and trap with phenylselenyl chloride. <oxidation> of the resulting alpha-selenyl <ketone> with <hydrogen> peroxide produces a selenoxide, whose syn elimination introduces the enone double bond on that side. Renumbering the double bond from the <carbonyl> gives **5-methylcyclohex-2-en-1-one A**. The selenium-containing phenyl group is a removable reagent fragment, not a <carbon> building block incorporated into A; the only new product <carbon> is the allowed methyl group. Regioselective kinetic <enolization> is important: enolizing the other alpha site would regenerate an enone with the methyl in the wrong position. This route produces racemic A unless a separate asymmetric step is introduced, which is sufficient for the stereochemically unspecified starting <ketone>.

= b
{parent=49}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Tin tetrachloride coordinates the enone <carbonyl>, lowering the dienophile pi-star energy. Butadiene reacts in an endo-oriented Diels–Alder <cycloaddition>: its terminal <carbons> bond to the enone's two <alkene> <carbons>, its central bond becomes the new cyclohexene double bond, and the enone C=C pi <electrons> become a new <sigma bond>. All three bond reorganizations occur in a <suprafacial> six-electron cycle.

Approach from the face **opposite the methyl substituent** avoids steric crowding and gives the indicated relationship between methyl and the non-enolizable junction <hydrogen>. The immediate stereospecific <cycloaddition> gives a cis-fused junction relationship. The junction <carbon> next to the <ketone> can subsequently enolize; reprotonation on either face epimerizes this centre without breaking either newly formed C–C bond. This accounts for the mixture at the wavy-bond position while the other junction and methyl relationship remains fixed. The modest 1.5:1 ratio should not be mistaken for failure of <suprafaciality> in the <cycloaddition> itself. Later oxime formation removes the <ketone>'s ordinary <enolization> pathway and furnishes the depicted isolated junction <diastereomer>.

= c
{parent=49}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The oxime is reduced at its C=N bond rather than cleaving N–O: <hydride> addition to the <imine> <carbon> followed by <protonation> gives an **N-substituted hydroxylamine**. In a chair-like model of the cis-fused ring system, the fused framework and methyl group make the two faces inequivalent. The favoured <hydride> trajectory accesses the face that places the newly installed C–H bond behind the original drawing and the retained **C–NHOH bond in front**, giving D. It avoids the more congested trajectory into the folded bicyclic framework; a flat hexagon comparison alone misses this facial distinction.

The existing methyl and junction centres are retained. The selectivity is therefore **diastereoselective substrate control**, not enantioselectivity from <sodium cyanoborohydride>, which is achiral. For a racemic starting bicycle the same relative stereochemical preference gives a racemic pair of D <enantiomers>.

= d
{parent=49}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The secondary hydroxylamine condenses with formaldehyde. <nitrogen> attacks the <carbonyl> <carbon>, giving a carbinolamine; proton transfer and loss of water generate the tethered <nitrone> **$\mathrm{R-N^+(O^-)=CH_2}$**, where $R$ is the entire bicyclic substituent attached to <nitrogen> in D. Molecular sieves remove water, favouring this condensation.

The <nitrone> undergoes an intramolecular <1,3-dipolar cycloaddition> with the retained cyclohexene double bond. To define E precisely, label the former oxime <carbon> $C_A$, the adjacent upper ring junction $C_B$, the other junction $C_C$, and the <alkene> <carbons> $C_D$ and $C_E$ such that the chain from $C_A$ to the more remote <alkene> terminus is $C_A-C_B-\mathrm{CH_2}-C_D=C_E-\mathrm{CH_2}-C_C$. Let $C_F$ be the new formaldehyde-derived <nitrone> methylene. The <cycloaddition> forms **$C_F-C_D$ and O–$C_E$**, retains N–O and N–$C_A$, and changes N=$C_F$ and $C_D=C_E$ into single bonds. The new five-membered heterocycle is **N–$C_F$–$C_D$–$C_E$–O**. These connections, together with the original bicycle, specify **the bridged <isoxazolidine> E**.

The inwardly tethered <cycloaddition> is <suprafacial> at the <alkene> and preserves the existing <carbon> <stereocentres>; the C–C and C–O additions occur on the same accessible <alkene> face. The pre-existing N-substituent orientation restricts which fold can bring both reacting ends together without crossing the bridge. This is a genuine two-new-bond <cycloaddition>, not a Mannich reaction followed by unrelated oxygenation.

\Image[paper-3-isoxazolidine.png]
{title=Original atom-labelled connectivity model of the nitrone cycloaddition product E}

= e
{parent=49}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

<methyl fluorosulfonate> alkylates the tertiary <isoxazolidine> <nitrogen>, giving a **quaternary N-methyl ammonium salt**; the methyl remains attached to <nitrogen> in F. <hydride> from <lithium aluminium hydride> then cleaves the N–O bond, releasing the corresponding <oxygen> as an aluminium-bound <alkoxide> while restoring neutral <tertiary amine> <nitrogen>. Aqueous workup protonates the <alkoxide> to give the <alcohol>.

Thus the C–O bond made in the <cycloaddition> survives as the alcohol-bearing C–O bond, and the formaldehyde-derived methylene survives as the extra <carbon> between <nitrogen> and the <carbon> framework. The N–O bond is the one removed. **N-methylation followed by reductive N–O cleavage gives the bridged amino <alcohol> F** without requiring cleavage of its newly formed C–C bond or hydrogenation of a nonexistent residual <alkene>.

